---
.title = "zgix — a zero-allocation L4/L7 edge proxy in Zig",
.layout = "index.shtml",
---

zgix is built on the TigerBeetle I/O model — completion-based, with
caller-owned completions — and held to TigerStyle: **all memory is reserved
at startup, and the request-serving path allocates nothing.** One thread
owns one event loop and every pool; scale-out is N independent processes
behind `SO_REUSEPORT`, never threads sharing memory.

Every limit is a compile-time constant, and the memory, file-descriptor and
ring-depth budgets are closed-form functions of them — printed at startup
and asserted against `RLIMIT_NOFILE` before a port is bound. When a
resource runs out, zgix sheds the newest work with a defined answer: a
static `503` from constant memory, never a crash, never an unbounded queue.

Correctness rests on a deterministic simulator: the serving path runs
against virtual sockets and a virtual clock under a seeded adversarial
backend — partial reads down to one byte, resets at every point, delayed
and black-holed connects. A live gate then runs the real binary against a
real origin on every change and asserts that N requests produce exactly N
access-log lines, that counters reconcile, that memory does not move, and
that `SIGTERM` drains cleanly.
